# M12 kernels/shift_var.c — the barrel shifter (sandwiched)
# libcqops L4 gate-count goldens. PIN COUNTS, NEVER TRACES
# (risk R5): a trace churns on D4 free-list order and D6
# non-demotion, neither of which changes the circuit.
#
# bennett: 980805de85314b3da7ac25cf6454b56566f8e609
# measured-at: ALL-QUANTUM on both the value and the amount — the amount being quantum is what separates M12 from M11; at ctrl_depth 0
#
# BOTH OF THOSE LINES ARE DATA, NOT DECORATION: cq_gold_open
# reads them back, and a checking run fails if either
# disagrees with the snapshot on disk or with what the suite
# declares. The mask matters because operand folds still fire
# (a^0 and x&0 legitimately emit fewer gates, which is what L5
# proves), so a count is a function of (W, operand mask) — and
# a golden that does not say which mask it was taken at pins
# nothing. The line above is per-file for the reason bd 2r5
# records: as a literal in the writer it was false in four of
# the twelve files and nothing could contradict it.
#
# WHY ALL-QUANTUM IS THE RIGHT MASK FOR A KERNEL GOLDEN: with
# no demotion (D6) a mask can only drift TOWARDS Q, so it is
# the FIXED POINT, and the one mask where forward and unc
# agree. A file measured anywhere else says so above, and owes
# the reader a note saying why.
#
# forward and unc are pinned SEPARATELY and their equality is
# NOT an invariant (Rule 14, PRD §10): _unc may legitimately
# emit MORE gates than the forward did, because a rotation on
# a source between the two materialises bits that were
# constants at forward time. Do not 'fix' a difference.
#
# Regenerate: CQOPS_UPDATE_GOLDENS=1 ctest --test-dir
# build-release -R kernel   (ctest does NOT forward trailing
# --args to test binaries, so the env var is the mechanism;
# --update-goldens works when running the binary directly.)
#
# kernel   pass      W   NOT  CNOT  Toffoli
shl_var   forward    1     0     1        0
shl_var   unc        1     0     1        0
shl_var   forward    2     0    20        4
shl_var   unc        2     0    20        4
